e #000000 6.8pt;border-left-style: none;border-right-style: none;border-top: solid #000000 1.1pt;padding-bottom: 0pt;padding-left: 0;padding-right: 0;padding-top: 0pt;vertical-align: middle;width: 75.85pt;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 8pt;line-height: 8.8pt;margin-bottom: 2.25pt;margin-right: 0.26in;margin-top: 2.15pt;text-align: right;vertical-align: baseline;"&gt;&lt;div style="color: #000000;font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 8pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;295,592,430&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="border-bottom: double #000000 6.8pt;border-left-style: none;border-right-style: none;border-top: solid #000000 1.1pt;padding-bottom: 0pt;padding-left: 0;padding-right: 0;padding-top: 0pt;vertical-align: middle;width: 72.25pt;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 8pt;line-height: 8.9pt;margin-bottom: 2.15pt;margin-right: 0.07in;margin-top: 2.15pt;text-align: right;vertical-align: baseline;"&gt;&lt;div style="color: #000000;font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 8pt;font-style: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-weight:bold;display:inline;"&gt;339,476,903&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;margin-bottom: 0;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;margin-bottom: 0;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;margin-bottom: 0;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-weight:bold;display:inline;"&gt;Basis of Presentation&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;The accompanying financial statements were prepared in conformity with the accounting principles prescribed by the FERC, as set forth in the applicable Uniform System of Accounts and published accounting releases, which is a comprehensive basis of accounting other than accounting principles generally accepted in the United States of America, (GAAP).&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-weight:bold;display:inline;"&gt;Use of Estimates&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;Management has made certain estimates and assumptions that affect the reported amounts of assets and liabilities and of income and expense and the disclosure of contingent assets and liabilities. Actual results can, and often do, differ from those estimates.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-weight:bold;display:inline;"&gt;Cash and Cash Equivalents&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;The Company considers short-term investments with an original maturity of less than three months to be cash equivalents.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 107.9%;margin-bottom: 8pt;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt; &lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-weight:bold;display:inline;"&gt;Government Regulation&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;The Company is subject to regulation by the FERC. The Company&amp;#8217;s accounting policies conform to the Financial Accounting Standards Codification (FASB ASC) 980, Regulated Operations. Accordingly, certain assets and liabilities that result from the regulated ratemaking process are recorded that would not be recorded under GAAP for nonregulated entities.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-weight:bold;display:inline;"&gt;Revenue Recognition&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;georgia&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;Transmission revenues are primarily generated from gas transmission through the Company&amp;#8217;s pipeline system under transportation service agreements which includes a two-part transportation fee consisting of reservation rate and a usage rate per volume (dekatherm) of natural gas transported. Depending on the nature of the agreement, other fees may include commodity fees and short-term balancing agreement fees (SBS fee) and charge for or credit for capacity release fees. Generally, for these contracts: (i) the promise is to transfer (or stand ready to transfer) natural gas as a service through the pipeline system over a period of time, which is a single performance obligation; (ii) the transaction price is derived from a tariff rate and includes fixed consideration which is determinable at contract inception and/or each month end based on the right to invoice at month end for the value of the monthly services provided to the customer; and (iii) the transaction price is recognized as revenue over the service period specified in the contract as the services are rendered. Transmission revenue also includes revenues earned from commodity and supply based services which are assessed based on volume through puts or nominations and billed on a monthly basis.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 107.9%;margin-bottom: 8pt;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="color: #000000;font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;letter-spacing: -0.05pt;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-weight:bold;display:inline;"&gt;Employee Benefits&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 11pt;line-height: 107.9%;margin-bottom: 8pt;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="color: #000000;font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;letter-spacing: -0.05pt;;display:inline;"&gt;The Company has no employees eligible to be enrolled in pension plans and other postretirement plans and therefore incurred no direct pension or other postretirement employee benefit costs. The Company does have indirect pension and &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;calibri&#39;;font-size: 11pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;other post retirement employee benefit costs from the work performed for it by affiliated companies&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="color: #000000;font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;letter-spacing: -0.05pt;;display:inline;"&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;a style="text-decoration: none;"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-weight:bold;display:inline;"&gt;Income Taxes&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;calibri&#39;;font-size: 11pt;margin-bottom: 0;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;calibri&#39;;font-size: 11pt;font-style: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-weight:bold;display:inline;"&gt;Pre-Merger&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;As a limited liability company, Spire MoGas Pipeline LLC is not a tax paying entity and taxable income is passed through to its members to be included in their federal and state income tax returns. In accordance with FERC requirements, the Company reports the hypothetical income tax effects of its transactions computed at statutory rates as if it were a tax-paying corporation. Deferred taxes are provided on all significant temporary differences between book and tax basis for assets and liabilities using the income tax rates in effect when it is expected that such differences will be realized.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;As a result of Spire MoGas Pipeline LLC current provision for taxes being funded directly by its member each year in which taxes are owed, the amount being recorded as a current provision for taxes will therefore not be paid.&amp;#160;&amp;#160;In lieu of reimbursement to the member for the taxes paid, the total tax obligation is recorded to equity as a member contribution during the following year. &lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;The provision for income taxes accounted for as if the Company was a corporation for FERC purposes consist of the following:&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="spacecount: 10;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;margin-bottom: 0;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="spacecount: 10;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="spacecount: 10;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;2025&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="spacecount: 10;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="spacecount: 10;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;2024&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;margin-bottom: 0;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="spacecount: 10;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="spacecount: 10;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;Current tax expense&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="spacecount: 10;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;$&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;1,797,325&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="spacecount: 10;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="spacecount: 10;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;$&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;2,122,020&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;margin-bottom: 0;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="spacecount: 10;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="spacecount: 10;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;Deferred tax expense&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="spacecount: 10;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;$&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;246,116&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="spacecount: 10;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;$&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;409,226&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="spacecount: 10;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;calibri&#39;;font-size: 11pt;margin-bottom: 0;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;calibri&#39;;font-size: 11pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;The tax effect of temporary differences that give rise to a significant portion of the deferred tax liabilities and assets at December 31, 2025 and 2024 are due to differences in basis, lives and methods of depreciation for property, plant and equipment, the tax deductibility of contingent liabilities and allowance for funds used during construction (&amp;#8220;AFUDC&amp;#8221;). In addition, the settlement of the rate case in September 2019 increased gas plant in service assets lives by just over 50% and required the recording of a regulatory liability of $464,594 to reflect the excess accumulated deferred income taxes (&amp;#8220;ADIT&amp;#8221;) resulting from the reduction in federal income tax rate from 35 percent to 21 percent derived from the Tax Cut and Jobs Act of 2017. This regulatory liability is amortized over 25 years at an annual amount of $18,590.&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;calibri&#39;;font-size: 11pt;margin-bottom: 0;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;calibri&#39;;font-size: 11pt;font-style: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-weight:bold;display:inline;"&gt;Spire STL Pipeline LLC (Post-Merger)&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;Spire STL Pipeline LLC accounts for income taxes under the asset and liability method.&amp;#160;&amp;#160;Deferred tax assets and liabilities are recognized for the future tax consequences attributable to differences between the financial statement carrying amount of existing assets and liabilities and the respective tax basis.&amp;#160;&amp;#160;Deferred tax assets and liabilities are measured using enacted tax rates expected to apply to taxable income in the years in which those temporary differences are expected to be realized or settled.&amp;#160;&amp;#160;The effects on deferred tax assets and liabilities of a change in enacted tax rates is recognized in the year of enactment.&amp;#160;&amp;#160;A valuation allowance is established when it is more likely than not that some portion or all the deferred tax assets will not be realized. The provisions of ASC 740, Income Taxes, are applied for the accounting for uncertainty in income taxes.&amp;#160;&amp;#160;This guidance addresses the determination of whether tax benefits claimed, or expected to be claimed, on a tax return should be recorded in the financial statements.&amp;#160;&amp;#160;The benefit from an uncertain tax position may be recognized only if it is more likely than not that the position will be sustained upon examination by the taxing authority, based on the technical merits of the position.&amp;#160;&amp;#160;Tax related interest and penalties, if any, are classified as a liability on the balance sheets. If interest or penalties are incurred, interest would be charged to interest expense and penalties as part of other income / expense.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 12.0pt;margin-bottom: 8pt;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="color: #000000;font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;There is a difference between the effective tax rate and the statutory tax rate primarily due to &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="color: #000000;font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;AFUDC&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="color: #000000;font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;. &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="color: #000000;font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;Deferred income taxes are provided on all temporary differences between the financial statement and tax basis of assets and liabilities. The significant temporary differences result primarily from property caused by differences in book and tax depreciation. Included in regulatory assets on the Company&amp;#8217;s balance sheet is the gross up of the deferred tax liability related to AFUDC. &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;The significant items comprising the net deferred tax liability or asset as of December 31, 2024 and 2023 were as follows:&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;margin-bottom: 0;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-weight:bold;display:inline;"&gt;Deferred tax assets:&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;margin-bottom: 0;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;Reserves not currently deductible $152,953&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;margin-bottom: 0;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;Total deferred tax assets $152,953&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;margin-bottom: 0;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;margin-bottom: 0;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-weight:bold;display:inline;"&gt;Deferred tax liabilities:&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;margin-bottom: 0;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;Relating to utility property $(28,201,778)&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;margin-bottom: 0;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;AFUDC debt and equity $(5,719,925)&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;margin-bottom: 0;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;Total deferred tax liabilities $(33,921,703)&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;margin-bottom: 0;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;Net deferred tax liability $(33,768,750)&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 12.0pt;margin-bottom: 0;margin-top: 0;text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 12.0pt;margin-bottom: 0;margin-top: 0;text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;Spire STL Pipeline LLC has no estimated federal and state loss carryforwards at December 31, 2025. For federal tax purposes, any loss carryforwards may be utilized against income from another member of the consolidated group. The Company recognizes the tax benefit from a tax position only if it is at least more likely than not that the tax position will be sustained on examination by the taxing authorities, based on the technical merits of the position.&amp;#160;&amp;#160;Spire STL Pipeline LLC had no unrecognized tax positions as of December 31, 2025. &lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 12.0pt;margin-bottom: 0;margin-top: 0;text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-weight:bold;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-weight:bold;display:inline;"&gt;Property, Plant and Equipment and Related Depreciation and Amortization&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;Property, plant and equipment are stated at original cost. Depreciation of pipeline facilities and compression equipment is provided on a straight-line composite basis over the estimated useful life of the pipeline of 61 years. Computer and software depreciation is provided on a straight-line basis over three years, while equipment, structures and leasehold improvements depreciation are provided on a straight-line basis over ten years. Repairs and maintenance costs are expensed as incurred. Costs that are considered betterments are capitalized. AFUDC, using the rate of return on the rate base approved by the FERC, is capitalized and included in the cost of property, plant and equipment. Amounts included in construction work in progress are not amortized until the assets are placed into service.&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-weight:bold;display:inline;"&gt;Impairment of Long-Lived Assets&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;The Company reviews long-lived assets such as property, plant and equipment for impairment whenever events or changes in circumstances indicate the carrying value may not be recoverable. No impairments have been recorded for the years ending December 31, 2025 and 2024.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-weight:bold;display:inline;"&gt;Fair Value of Financial Instruments&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;The carrying amounts of cash and cash equivalents, accounts receivable, accounts payable, accrued expenses, customer deposits and due from (to) related parties approximate fair value due to their short term to maturity.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;a style="text-decoration: none;"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-weight:bold;display:inline;"&gt;Accounts Receivable &lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;Accounts receivable are presented net of expected credit losses at estimated net realizable value by maintaining an allowance for uncollectible accounts. The current expected credit loss (&amp;#8220;CECL model&amp;#8221;) is applied to receivables for purposes of calculating the allowance for uncollectible accounts. This model is based on expected losses and results in the recognition of estimated expected credit losses, including uncollectible amounts for both billed and unbilled revenues, over the life of the receivable at the time a receivable is recorded. The allowance for uncollectible accounts is determined based upon a variety of judgments and factors, including the application of an estimated uncollectible percentage to each receivable aging category. Factors in determining credit loss include historical collection, write-off experience, and management&#39;s assessment of collectability from customers, including current conditions, reasonable forecasts, and expectations of future collectability and collection efforts. Management continuously assesses the collectability of receivables and adjusts estimates based on actual experience and future expectations based on economic indicators, collection efforts and other factors. Management also monitors the aging analysis of receivables to determine if there are changes in the collections of accounts receivable. Receivable balances are written off against the allowance for uncollectible accounts when the customer accounts are no longer in service and these balances are deemed to be uncollectible. There is no allowance for credit losses at December 31, 2025 and 2024.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="color: #000000;font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-weight:bold;display:inline;"&gt;Allowance For Funds Used During Construction&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="color: #000000;font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;Allowance for Funds Used During Construction (&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="color: #000000;font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;AFUDC&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="color: #000000;font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;)&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="color: #000000;font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt; is a non-cash item that represents the allowed cost of capital, including a return on equity, used to finance &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="color: #000000;font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;the Company&amp;#8217;s pipeline infrastructure &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="color: #000000;font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;capital projects. The portion of AFUDC attributable to borrowed funds or AFUDC debt is recorded as a reduction of interest&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="color: #000000;font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt; &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="color: #000000;font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;expense and the remainder is recorded as other income or AFUDC Equity. For the &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="color: #000000;font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;12 months &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="color: #000000;font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;ended December 31, 20&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="color: #000000;font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;25&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="color: #000000;font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;, &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="color: #000000;font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;there was no AFUDC capitalization&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-weight:bold;display:inline;"&gt;Asset Retirement Obligation&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 107.9%;margin-bottom: 8pt;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;a style="text-decoration: none;"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;The Company records asset retirement obligations for the present value of liabilities of future costs expected to be incurred when natural gas transmission pipelines are retired from service if the retirement process is legally required and if a reasonable estimate of fair value can be made. The Company also records asset retirement obligations for legal obligations that can be reasonably estimated, even if performance is conditional upon a future event.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 107.9%;margin-bottom: 8pt;margin-top: 6pt;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;The Company records the estimated retirement cost over the life of the related asset by depreciating the present value of the obligation (measured at the time of the asset&amp;#8217;s acquisition) and accreting the discount until the liability is settled. At December 31, 2025 the recorded asset retirement obligation was $438,290.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;margin-bottom: 0;margin-top: 0;text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-weight:bold;display:inline;"&gt;Business concentration and credit risk&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;margin-bottom: 0;margin-top: 0;text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;Financial instruments which potentially subject the Company to concentrations of credit risk consist primarily of cash and cash equivalents and accounts receivable. The Company maintains its cash with major U.S. banks. These amounts, at times, may exceed the federally insured limits. The terms of deposits are on demand to minimize risk. The Company has not incurred losses related to these deposits. Trade accounts receivable consist of uncollateralized receivables from customers. For the years ended December 31, 2025 and 2024, the Company had sales to three customers, which accounted for approximately 90% of total revenue. &lt;/div&gt;&lt;a style="text-decoration: none;"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;As of December 31, 2025 and 2024, accounts receivable from two and three customers account for approximately 81% and 79% of total accounts receivable (pre-merger), respectively.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-weight:bold;display:inline;"&gt;Related Party Transactions &lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;The Company regularly conducts business with related parties that are related through common ownership.&amp;#160;&amp;#160;During the years ending December 31, 2025 and 2024, sales to related parties were $9,677,323 and $9,267,836, respectively. The Company also rents warehouse and office space from a related party.&amp;#160;&amp;#160;Rental expense to the related party during the years ended December 31, 2025 and 2024 was $243,707 and $250,941, respectively. In addition, the Company has a shared services agreement with its member. Expenses allocated to the Company from the member for the Company&amp;#8217;s respective portion during the years ending December 31, 2025 and 2024 totaled $1,132,323 and $890,692, respectively.&amp;#160;&amp;#160;Accounts receivable to related parties as of December 31, 2025 and 2024 totaled $867,451 and $1,095,436, respectively (pre-merger). Accounts payable to related parties as of December 31, 2025 and 2024 totaled $961,712 and $372,229, respectively (pre-merger). At December 31, 2025 (post-merger), accounts receivable to related parties totaled $3,693,031 and accounts payable to related parties totaled $1,517,380 after including balances related to Spire STL Pipeline LLC.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-weight:bold;display:inline;"&gt;Financing Arrangements and Long-Term Debt&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;The Company has entered into an intercompany revolving credit agreement with Spire Inc. that allows the Company to borrow from Spire Inc. when necessary to meet working capital needs. The interest rate payable under the intercompany revolving credit agreement was 4.92% as of December 31, 2025. The notes payable balance to Spire Inc. was $13,382,004 as of December 31, 2025, the entire balance relating to STL Pipeline as part of the merger consolidation.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;On December 23, 2019, Spire STL Pipeline LLC issued and sold notes to certain institutional investors in a $135,000 private placement. Interest is payable semi-annually at 2.95%, and principal repayment is scheduled annually in accordance with a 15-year amortization schedule with an average life of 9.2 years. Proceeds were used to repay short-term debt. As of December 31, 2025, the notes had an outstanding balance of $96,552,000. Debt issuance costs for the 2019 notes were $833,791 and are recorded as an asset on the balance sheet. Maturities of long-term debt for the Company for the five calendar years and thereafter as of December 31, 2025 are as follows:&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;calibri&#39;;font-size: 11pt;margin-bottom: 0;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;calibri&#39;;font-size: 11pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;2026&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 11pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="spacecount: 10;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 11pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="spacecount: 10;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;calibri&#39;;font-size: 11pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;$8,059,500&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;calibri&#39;;font-size: 11pt;margin-bottom: 0;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;calibri&#39;;font-size: 11pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;2027&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 11pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="spacecount: 10;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 11pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="spacecount: 10;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;calibri&#39;;font-size: 11pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;$8,626,500&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;calibri&#39;;font-size: 11pt;margin-bottom: 0;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;calibri&#39;;font-size: 11pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;2028&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 11pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="spacecount: 10;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 11pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="spacecount: 10;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;calibri&#39;;font-size: 11pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;$9,234,000&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;calibri&#39;;font-size: 11pt;margin-bottom: 0;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;calibri&#39;;font-size: 11pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;2029&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 11pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="spacecount: 10;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 11pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="spacecount: 10;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;calibri&#39;;font-size: 11pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;$9,882,000&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;calibri&#39;;font-size: 11pt;margin-bottom: 0;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;calibri&#39;;font-size: 11pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;2030&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 11pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="spacecount: 10;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 11pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="spacecount: 10;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;calibri&#39;;font-size: 11pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;$10,570,500&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;calibri&#39;;font-size: 11pt;margin-bottom: 0;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;calibri&#39;;font-size: 11pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;Thereafter&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 11pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="spacecount: 10;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;calibri&#39;;font-size: 11pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;$50,179,500&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;calibri&#39;;font-size: 11pt;margin-bottom: 0;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 11pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 107.9%;margin-bottom: 8pt;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;Spire STL Pipeline&amp;#8217;s long-term debt agreement contains customary financial covenants and default provisions. As of December 31, 2025, there were no events of default under these financial covenants. The debt agreement was transferred to Spire MoGas Pipeline LLC, the merger surviving entity on December 31, 2025.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-weight:bold;display:inline;"&gt;Environmental Matters&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;The Company is subject to federal, state and local laws and regulations governing environmental quality and pollution control. These laws and regulations require the Company to remove and remedy the effect of the disposal or release of specified substances at current and former operating sites.&amp;#160;&amp;#160;Liabilities for environmental costs are recorded when it is probable that obligations have been incurred and the amounts can be reasonably estimated. The Company is not aware of any material environmental liabilities that should be recorded with respect to compliance with applicable laws and regulations. &lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 11pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="color: #000000;font-family: &#39;georgia&#39;;font-size: 11pt;font-style: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-weight:bold;display:inline;"&gt; &lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-weight:bold;display:inline;"&gt;Litigation and Legal Proceedings&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 107.9%;margin-bottom: 8pt;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;The Company is involved in legal and regulatory proceedings regarding matters arising in the ordinary course of business, which involve management&amp;#8217;s assessment to determine the probability of whether a loss will occur and, if probable, its best estimate of probable loss. The Company records and discloses losses when these are probable and reasonably estimable and discloses matters when losses are probable but not estimable or when losses are reasonably possible. Legal costs related to the defense of loss contingencies are expensed as incurred. Legal costs related to the FERC certification processes and efforts to maintain the certificates are capitalized.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-weight:bold;display:inline;"&gt;Subsequent Events&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;line-height: 115.0%;margin-bottom: 10pt;margin-top: 0;text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;;font-size: 10pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;Management evaluates subsequent events through the date financial statements are available for issue, which is the date of the Independent Auditor&amp;#8217;s Report, and has determined that there are no subsequent events requiring disclosure as of that date.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="font-family: &#39;calibri&#39;;font-size: 11pt;line-height: 107.9%;margin-bottom: 8pt;margin-top: 0;"&gt;&lt;div style="font-size: 11pt;font-style: ;font-weight: ;;display:inline;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;         
&lt;/div&gt;
</ferc:NotesToFinancialStatementsTextBlock>
